---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Knowledge — Jędrzej Szymula"
  description="SEO, AEO, and GEO explained — plain-language definitions and practical notes by Jędrzej Szymula."
  path="/knowledge.html"
  active="knowledge"
  ogTitle="Knowledge — SEO, AEO & GEO by Jędrzej Szymula"
  ogDescription="Three entries: Search Engine Optimization, Answer Engine Optimization, and Generative Engine Optimization."
>

  <div class="section-label">Three Entries</div>

  <!-- Entry 1: SEO -->
  <article class="entry" id="seo">
    <div class="entry-header">
      <span class="entry-word">SEO</span>
    </div>
    <p class="entry-definition">
      <strong>Search Engine Optimization.</strong> The practice of shaping a website's content, structure, and
      authority so it ranks well in traditional search results — Google, Bing, and the blue-link results pages
      that still drive the majority of the internet's traffic. SEO rests on three pillars:
      <span data-def="Crawlability, indexation, site speed, and the infrastructure that lets search engines read a site correctly">technical foundations</span>,
      <span data-def="Content built around what real people actually search for and why">content aligned to search intent</span>,
      and <span data-def="Earning links and mentions from other credible sites to build topical and domain authority">authority built through links and mentions</span>.
      Done well, it compounds — a page published today can still be sending qualified traffic in three years.
    </p>
    <p class="entry-example">
      "SEO is still the highest-leverage channel in digital marketing: it's the only one that keeps paying out after you stop actively working on it."
    </p>
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag">Technical SEO</span>
      <span class="tag">Content Strategy</span>
      <span class="tag">Link Building</span>
      <span class="tag">Foundational</span>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Entry 2: AEO -->
  <article class="entry" id="aeo">
    <div class="entry-header">
      <span class="entry-word">AEO</span>
    </div>
    <p class="entry-definition">
      <strong>Answer Engine Optimization.</strong> The practice of structuring content so it can be lifted directly
      into a machine-generated answer — featured snippets, "People also ask" boxes, voice assistant replies, and
      AI answer boxes. AEO grew out of the shift toward zero-click search: when the answer engine can extract a
      clean, self-contained answer from your page, it doesn't always need to send the user to your site at all.
      In practice this means writing in direct, extractable blocks — clear questions followed by concise answers,
      well-labeled steps, and structured data that removes ambiguity about what a page is actually saying.
    </p>
    <p class="entry-example">
      "If a search engine has to guess what your answer is, it will usually just answer for you — using someone else's page."
    </p>
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag">Featured Snippets</span>
      <span class="tag">Voice Search</span>
      <span class="tag">Structured Data</span>
      <span class="tag">Zero-Click</span>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Entry 3: GEO -->
  <article class="entry" id="geo">
    <div class="entry-header">
      <span class="entry-word">GEO</span>
    </div>
    <p class="entry-definition">
      <strong>Generative Engine Optimization.</strong> The practice of structuring content and digital presence so
      that generative AI platforms — ChatGPT, Google AI Overviews, Gemini, Perplexity — cite, recommend, or mention
      a brand when users ask a related question. Unlike traditional SEO, the "result" isn't a ranked link; it's a
      sentence inside a synthesized answer, often with no click at all. Because these models draw heavily on
      third-party sources — reviews, forums, comparison sites, documentation — being cited well depends on being
      clearly and consistently described across the wider web, not just on-site.
      SEO, AEO, and GEO overlap heavily in practice: clear structure, genuine expertise, and credible external
      mentions serve all three at once.
    </p>
    <p class="entry-example">
      "The brand doesn't need to win every click anymore — it needs to be the answer the model reaches for."
    </p>
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag">AI Search</span>
      <span class="tag">LLM Citations</span>
      <span class="tag">Brand Mentions</span>
      <span class="tag">Emerging</span>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
